{{also|EY|-ey|-ey-|eþ}}
==English==

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|ey}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ǣġ}} ("egg"; {{m|ang|ǣġru}} in the plural), from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*ajją}}, {{m|gem-pro|*ajjaz||egg}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*h₂ōwyóm|t=egg}}. Cognate with {{cog|stq|Oai||egg}}, {{cog|fy|aai||egg}}, {{cog|nl|ei||egg}}, {{cog|nds-de|Ei||egg}}, {{cog|de|Ei||egg}}, {{cog|da|æg||egg}}, {{cog|sv|ägg||egg}}, {{cog|is|egg||egg}}, {{cog|gd|ugh||egg}}, {{cog|la|ōvum||egg}}. Was displaced by {{m|en|egg}} in the 16th century, most likely due to its clashing with the word "[[eye]]", with which it had come to be a [[homonym]].

====Noun====
{{en-noun|eyren}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} An [[egg]]. {{defdate|obsolete since the sixteenth century}}
#* {{quote-book|year=1490|author=William Caxton|title=Prologue to ''Eneydos''
|passage=And one of theym... cam in to an hows and axed for mete and specyally he axyd after ''eggys'', and the goode wyf answerde that she could speke no Frenshe. And the marchaunt was angry, for he also coude speke no Frenshe, but wolde have hadde ''egges''; and she understode hym not. And thenne at laste a-nother sayd that he wolde have '''''eyren'''''. Then the good wyf sayd that she understod hym wel. Loo, what sholde a man in thyse dayes now wryte, ''egges'', or '''''eyren'''''? Certaynly it is hard to playse every man, by-cause of dyversite and chaunge of langage.}}
#* {{quote-text|year=1787|title=Liber quotidianus contrarotulatoris garderobae|passage=Take brothe of capons withoute herbes, and breke '''eyren''', and cast into the pot, and make a crudde therof, and colour hit with saffron, and then presse oute the brothe and kerve it on leches; and then take swete creme of almondes, or of cowe mylk, and boyle hit; {{...}}}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|Cockney}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|eye}}, {{m|enm|eie}}, {{m|enm|eȝe|t=fear, terror}}, from {{inh|en|ang|eġe|t=fear, terror}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*agaz|t=fear, dread}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₂egʰ-|t=to be depressed, afraid}}. In all southern and most northern dialects merged with [[awe]] completely. See also [[beseek]] and [[beseech]]; [[thrack]] and [[thrutch]]; [[give]] and [[yive]]; [[streek]] and [[stretch]].

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete|regional|rare}} [[fear|Fear]], [[terror]].
#: {{ux|en|To have no '''ey''' for nought.}}
#* '''c1470''', ''O lord omnipotent''ː
#*: Exhorting thy people to have a special '''ey''', That thee to praise they never cease.
# {{lb|en|obsolete|regional|rare}} A feeling of fear and [[reverence]].

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete|regional|rare}} To [[awe]].

